{{Politics of Malawi}}

General elections were held in [[Malawi]] on 16 September 2025 to elect the [[President of Malawi|president]], the 229 members of the [[National Assembly (Malawi)|National Assembly]] and 509 local government councillors.<ref>{{cite web |title=What is at stake in Malawi's elections? |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckgy3g7zpl3o |website=BBC |date=1 September 2025 |access-date=1 September 2025}}</ref> 

The presidential election resulted in a victory for former president [[Peter Mutharika]] of the [[Democratic Progressive Party (Malawi)|Democratic Progressive Party]], who received 57% of the vote. Incumbent president [[Lazarus Chakwera]] of the [[Malawi Congress Party]] finished second with 33% of the vote.

==Background==
The election was the second conducted under the [[Two-round system|two-round]] constitutional threshold introduced after the annulment of the [[2019 Malawian general election]] and the subsequent [[2020 Malawian presidential election|fresh presidential election]] the following year that brought [[Lazarus Chakwera]] to power. In July 2024, the [[United Transformation Movement]] (UTM) withdrew from the governing [[Tonse Alliance (Malawi)|Tonse Alliance]], leaving the [[Malawi Congress Party]] (MCP) to contest the elections on its own.<ref name="auto2">{{cite web |title=Fragments of opinion after UTM quit the Tonse Alliance |url=https://malawi24.com/2024/07/13/fragments-of-opinion-after-utm-quit-the-tonse-alliance/ |website=Malawi24 |date=13 July 2024 |access-date=26 June 2025}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The president of Malawi is elected using a [[two-round system]]. Should no candidate secure an absolute majority in the first round, a run-off is held within 30 days. Members of the National Assembly are elected in single-member constituencies by [[first-past-the-post voting]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Malawi: Electoral system |url=http://archive.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2195_B.htm |website=Inter-Parliamentary Union |access-date=26 June 2025}}</ref> 

According to the [[Malawi Electoral Commission]], 7.2 million voters were enrolled after the three registration phases, with women constituting 57% of the electorate.<ref>{{cite web |title=4 million women register to vote in 2025 |url=https://mwnation.com/4m-women-register-to-vote-in-2025/ |website=The Nation |date=24 December 2024 |access-date=26 June 2025}}</ref> At 65%, the number of eligible voters who enrolled was less from the 80% recorded in 2019.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Malawians vote for president with their southern African nation gripped by economic turmoil |url=https://apnews.com/article/election-malawi-president-parliament-africa-chakwera-397aa544946efd6e1dfc0258d406bfdf |access-date=16 September 2025 |website=AP News |date=16 September 2025 }}</ref>

==Parties and alliances==
*[[Malawi Congress Party]] (MCP){{snd}}governing party of President Chakwera; running without the UTM following the collapse of the [[Tonse Alliance (Malawi)|Tonse Alliance]] in 2024.<ref name="auto2"/>
*[[Democratic Progressive Party (Malawi)|Democratic Progressive Party]] (DPP){{snd}}main opposition party; endorsed former president [[Peter Mutharika]] as its presidential candidate in August 2024.<ref name="auto1">{{cite news |title=Malawi's main opposition endorses ex-president Mutharika to run in 2025 |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/malawis-main-opposition-endorses-ex-president-mutharika-run-2025-2024-08-18/ |website=Reuters |date=18 August 2024 |access-date=26 June 2025}}</ref> 
*[[People's Party (Malawi)|People's Party]] (PP){{snd}}led by former president [[Joyce Banda]], who relaunched her national campaign in June 2025.<ref name="auto">{{cite web |title=Joyce Banda says Malawi needs a new vision |url=https://allafrica.com/stories/202506230359.html |website=Nyasa Times|via=AllAfrica |date=23 June 2025 |access-date=26 June 2025}}</ref>
*[[United Democratic Front (Malawi)|United Democratic Front]] (UDF){{snd}}headed by [[Atupele Muluzi]], which has indicated it may field its own ticket if no opposition alliance is formed.<ref>{{cite web |title=Atupele Muluzi accuses DPP of succession fears ahead of 2025 elections |url=https://malawi24.com/2025/06/22/atupele-muluzi-accuses-dpp-of-succession-fears-ahead-of-2025-elections/ |website=Malawi24 |date=22 June 2025 |access-date=26 June 2025}}</ref>
*[[United Transformation Movement]] (UTM){{snd}}Following the death of founder [[Saulos Chilima]], Vice-President [[Michael Usi]] became interim leader as per the party's constitution. He initially expressed interest in leading the party,<ref>{{cite web |title=Michael Usi eyes UTM presidency |url=https://times.mw/michael-usi-eyes-utm-presidency/ |website=Times Group |date=21 October 2024 |access-date=26 June 2025}}</ref> but did not contest at the November 2024 convention,<ref>{{cite web |title=Analysis: Michael Usi's decision to withdraw from UTM's Elective Convention |url=https://malawi24.com/2024/11/11/analysis-michael-usis-decision-to-withdraw-from-utms-elective-convention/ |website=Malawi24 |date=11 November 2024 |access-date=31 July 2025}}</ref> where [[Dalitso Kabambe]] was elected as the new party leader.<ref>{{cite web |title=Dalitso Kabambe wins landslide victory at UTM Convention |url=https://malawi24.com/2024/11/18/dalitso-kabambe-wins-landslide-victory-at-utm-convention/ |website=Malawi24 |date=18 November 2024 |access-date=31 July 2025}}</ref>

==Candidates==
===Declared===
*[[Lazarus Chakwera]]{{snd}}was the incumbent president; endorsed by the MCP in August 2024.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chakwera secures MCP backing for 2025 race |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/malawi-president-chakwera-secures-his-partys-support-second-term-bid-2024-08-09/ |website=Reuters |date=9 August 2024 |access-date=26 June 2025}}</ref> His running mate was [[Vitumbiko Mumba]], the former minister of trade and industry and former labour minister, who was chosen despite MCP constitutional norms that typically favour the First Deputy President.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chitete |first=Suzgo |date=2025-07-31 |title=Minister Mumba emerges as Chakwera's chosen one |url=https://mwnation.com/minister-mumba-emerges-as-chakweras-chosen-one/ |access-date=2025-09-25 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref>
*[[Peter Mutharika]]{{snd}}former president (2014–2020); nominated by the DPP.<ref name="auto1"/> His running mate was [[Jane Ansah]], the former [[Malawi Electoral Commission]] chairperson.<ref>{{cite web |title=I'm Back to Rescue Malawi" — Mutharika Declares as He Unveils Jane Ansah as Running Mate |url=https://www.nyasatimes.com/im-back-to-rescue-malawi-mutharika-declares-as-he-unveils-jane-ansah-as-running-mate/ |website=Nyasatimes |date=25 July 2025 |access-date=30 July 2025}}</ref>
*[[Joyce Banda]]{{snd}}former president (2012–2014); announced her candidacy on 23 June 2025.<ref name="auto"/> Her running mate was [[Khumbo Kachali]], her former vice‑president during her 2012–2014 term.<ref>{{cite web |title=Former President Joyce Banda unveils her former Vice-President Khumbo Kachali as her running mate for the September 16 election |url=https://www.maraviexpress.com/former-president-joyce-banda-unveils-her-former-vice-president-khumbo-kachali-as-her-running-mate-for-the-september-16-election/ |website=Malavi Express |date=24 July 2025 |access-date=26 July 2025}}</ref>
*[[Dalitso Kabambe]]{{snd}}former governor of the [[Reserve Bank of Malawi]] and candidate of the UTM party. His running mate was [[Matthews Mtumbuka]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Dalitso Kabambe Picks Mathews Mtumbuka as Running Mate Ahead of September 16 Presidential Poll |url=https://malawifreedomnetwork.com/2025/07/27/dalitso-kabambe-picks-mathews-mtumbuka-as-running-mate-ahead-of-september-16-presidential-poll/?srsltid=AfmBOorv6pFYiUCqv-raamxAiRfmx5E0DqNvwWVAGCaNhNvfb9DnQpcE |website=Malawi Freedom Network |date=27 July 2025 |access-date=31 July 2025}}</ref>
*[[Atupele Muluzi]]{{snd}}UDF president. His running mate was Dr. Rex Kalolo.<ref>{{cite web |title=I'm not backing anyone{{snd}}Muluzi |url=https://malawi24.com/2025/07/26/im-not-backing-anyone-muluzi/ |website=Malawi24 |date=26 July 2025 |access-date=31 July 2025}}</ref>

==Campaign==
The official campaign period began on 14 July 2025 at the [[Bingu International Conference Centre]] (BICC) in Lilongwe, with activities continuing through 14 September, two days before polling on 16 September 2025, according to the MEC election calendar.<ref>{{cite web |title=Malawi's official campaign season begins as MEC opens 60-day window |url=https://malawi24.com/2025/07/14/malawis-official-campaign-season-begins-as-mec-opens-60-day-window/ |website=Malawi24 |date=14 July 2025 |access-date=5 August 2025}}</ref>

=== Presidential debates ===
The Presidential Debates Taskforce, chaired by MISA Malawi, organised debates ahead of the 2025 general election with support from the National Democratic Institute. The first debate was held on 21 August 2025 at the BICC and broadcast nationally.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Malawi's 2025 Presidential Debates: Dates, Parties and What to Expect |url=https://www.zodiakmalawi.com/politics/malawis-2025-presidential-debates-dates-parties-and-what-to-expect |access-date=3 September 2025 |website=www.zodiakmalawi.com}}</ref> A second debate was rescheduled to 9 September 2025. President [[Lazarus Chakwera]] and former president [[Peter Mutharika]] did not participate.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mhone |first=Macmillan |date=21 August 2025 |title=Chakwera, APM shun presidential debate |url=https://mwnation.com/chakwera-apm-shun-presidential-debate/ |access-date=3 September 2025 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Peace initiative by the Public Affairs Committee ===
On 8 September 2025, the [[Public Affairs Committee (Malawi)|Public Affairs Committee]] (PAC) held a National Day of Prayers and Peace Declaration signing at the BICC in Lilongwe. The event brought together several presidential candidates, including President Lazarus Chakwera, Joyce Banda, Atupele Muluzi, and Dalitso Kabambe, who pledged to promote peaceful elections.<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 September 2025 |title=Chakwera Leads Rivals in Smiles and Handshakes at Peace Prayers — But Usi and Mutharika Nowhere to Be Seen |work=Nyasa Times |url=https://www.nyasatimes.com/chakwera-leads-rivals-in-smiles-and-handshakes-at-peace-prayers-but-usi-and-mutharika-nowhere-to-be-seen/ |access-date=9 September 2025 |language=en-GB}}</ref> 

The gathering was marked by gestures of unity. Peter Mutharika and Michael Usi's absence drew criticism.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Njiragoma |first=Wycliffe |date=9 September 2025 |title=APM, others shun peace pact event |url=https://mwnation.com/apm-others-shun-peace-pact-event/ |access-date=9 September 2025 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=8 September 2025 |title=DPP and Odya Zake Humiliated Barred from Signing Peace Pact Because Their Leaders Didn't Show Up |work=Nyasa Times|url=https://www.nyasatimes.com/dpp-and-odya-zake-humiliated-barred-from-signing-peace-pact-because-their-leaders-didnt-show-up/ |access-date=9 September 2025 |language=en-GB}}</ref> PAC chairperson Patrick Thawale stressed that the declaration would help manage disputes during and after the elections.<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 September 2025 |title=Presidential Candidates Successfully Sign Peace Declaration-Crucial Step Towards 2025 General Elections |work=Public Affairs Committee |url=https://pacmw.org/presidential-candidates-successfully-sign-peace-declaration-crucial-step-towards-2025-general-elections/ |access-date=15 September 2025 |language=en-US}}</ref>

