Another poll conducted by [[Afrobarometer]] in August 2024 showed that 43% of respondents supported the [[Democratic Progressive Party (Malawi)|Democratic Progressive Party]], 29% the [[Malawi Congress Party]], 7% the [[United Transformation Movement]], and 2% the [[United Democratic Front (Malawi)|United Democratic Front]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Survey result favours DPP |url=https://times.mw/survey-result-favours-dpp/ |website=Times Group |date=7 December 2024 |access-date=12 August 2025}}</ref>

==Conduct==
Civil society and media advocates such as MISA Malawi have called for unrestricted press freedom and safety for journalists, citing prior instances of intimidation during electoral periods.<ref>{{cite web |title=MISA Malawi calls for free media ahead of 2025 Malawi elections |url=https://malawi.misa.org/2024/05/03/misa-malawi-calls-for-free-media-ahead-of-2025-malawi-elections/ |website=Misa Malawi |date=3 May 2024 |access-date=5 August 2025}}</ref> In June 2025, a rally calling for the resignation of top officials of the [[Malawi Electoral Commission]] (MEC) was attacked by masked, machete-wielding men.<ref>{{cite web |title=Malawi votes for next president as cost-of-living bites |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckgy3g7zpl3o |website=BBC |date=16 September 2025 |access-date=16 September 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Singini |first=George |date=27 June 2025 |title=Police, MDF fail to protect demonstrators |url=https://mwnation.com/police-mdf-fail-to-protect-demonstrators/ |access-date=16 September 2025 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref> 
MEC engaged six international observer missions, undertook gender‑sensitive and risk‑management training, and facilitated party briefings on voter roll verification to reinforce preparedness and credibility.<ref>{{cite web |title=MEC Engages Six Pre-Election Observer Missions Ahead of 2025 General Election |url=https://mec.org.mw/2025/06/24/mec-engages-six-pre-election-observer-missions-ahead-of-2025-general-election/ |website=Malawi Electoral Commission |date=24 June 2025 |access-date=5 August 2025}}</ref>

On 29 August 2025, MEC completed the accreditation of election observers for the 16 September general election, approving 19 international observers and 40 local observers.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MEC accredits 19 international observers and 40 locals for September 16 general election |work=Maravi Express |url=https://www.maraviexpress.com/mec-accredits-19-international-observers-and-40-locals-for-september-16-general-election/ |access-date=18 September 2025 |language=en-US}}</ref> 

On 27 August 2025, MEC conducted a dry-run of its Results Management System (RMS) ahead of the general election. The exercise, held at the national tally centre in [[Lilongwe]] and in selected constituencies, aimed to test the speed, accuracy, and security of results transmission.<ref>{{cite web |title=MEC conducts results system dry run |url=https://times.mw/mec-conducts-results-system-dry-run/ |website=Times Group |date=28 August 2025 |access-date=30 August 2025}}</ref> The test revealed some challenges, including network glitches in [[Blantyre]] and Lilongwe that delayed the transmission of results. MEC acknowledged the issues but stated that the system's integrity remained intact and pledged to resolve the problems before polling day.<ref>{{cite web |title=Network glitches mar MEC system test |url=https://mwnation.com/network-glitches-mar-mec-system-test/ |website=Nation Publications Limited |date=28 August 2025 |access-date=30 August 2025}}</ref>

=== Results management and observer concerns ===
In their preliminary report, international and regional observer missions, including the [[European Union]] and [[Southern African Development Community]], urged MEC to ensure transparency in the management of election results. The missions, along with several civil society groups, expressed concern about delays in publishing partial results and called for safeguarding election data and materials against tampering or loss.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Njiragoma |first=Wycliffe |date=2025-09-19 |title=EU observers point out uneven playing field |url=https://mwnation.com/eu-observers-point-out-uneven-playing-field/ |access-date=2025-09-19 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Malawi election observers urge transparent election results management |url=https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/malawi-election-observers-urge-transparent-election-results-management/3691680 |access-date=18 September 2025 |website=Anadolu Ajansi}}</ref>

On 18 September 2025, some political party representatives raised concerns after tally kits from districts including [[Nkhata Bay District|Nkhata Bay]], [[Luchenza]], and [[Kasungu District|Kasungu]] arrived at the national tally centre in [[Lilongwe]] without proper security seals. The handover was briefly halted, with MEC pledging to investigate; it later resumed after parties received printouts from the devices for verification.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Njiragoma |first=Wycliffe |date=2025-09-19 |title=Parties tussle over unsealed vote tally kits |url=https://mwnation.com/parties-tussle-over-unsealed-vote-tally-kits/ |access-date=2025-09-19 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref> 

On 21 September, MEC chairperson [[Annabel Mtalimanja]] cautioned political parties against making unverified claims of victory during the tallying process, saying that premature declarations can erode public trust and threaten national peace.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Njiragoma |first=Wycliffe |date=2025-09-22 |title=MEC cautions parties against victory claims |url=https://mwnation.com/392002-2/ |access-date=2025-09-22 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Irregularities and investigations ===
On 19 September, the MCP said it had found evidence of irregularities in the vote count in 13 of Malawi's 28 districts. Separately, police arrested eight election data entry clerks in the Lilongwe area on suspicion of "manipulating data".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-09-20 |title=Malawi election result: Eight arrested for alleged electoral fraud |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cx239dj0yx7o |access-date=2025-09-20 |website=BBC |language=en-GB}}</ref> An investigation was also launched into the alleged suicide attempt of an election returning officer who had offered bribes in exchange for manipulating the election results. Four domestic broadcasters also stopped live dashboards of their unofficial voting tallies without explanation that same day.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20250919-malawi-ruling-party-claims-tampering-in-vote-count |title=Malawi ruling party claims tampering in vote count |work=France 24 |date=20 September 2025|access-date=20 September 2025}}</ref> 

On 20 September, [[United Democratic Front (Malawi)|United Democratic Front]] (UDF) president and presidential candidate [[Atupele Muluzi]] claimed there was vote‐rigging in the party's stronghold districts, citing [[Mangochi District|Mangochi]] and [[Balaka Township, Malawi|Balaka]] as some of the areas where serious irregularities occurred. He also alleged that many UDF monitors were directed to leave polling centers and told to return only after results had already been tallied in order to sign the official record sheets. He called for investigations into the matter and urged that all votes cast be properly accounted for.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-09-20 |title=Atupele Muluzi claims vote rigging in stronghold districts |url=https://www.malawivoice.com/2025/09/20/atupele-muluzi-claims-vote-rigging-in-stronghold-districts/ |access-date=2025-09-21 |website=Malawi Voice |language=en-GB}}</ref>

Civil society organisations, through the Civil Society Elections Integrity Forum (CSEIF), on 20 September called for a speedy and transparent investigation into the recent arrests of electoral officials and clerks in Lilongwe Nkhoma Constituency. Separate inquiries were also reported in Mangochi, [[Chikwawa District|Chikwawa]], [[Blantyre District|Blantyre]], [[Machinga District|Machinga]], and [[Rumphi District|Rumphi]], highlighting the broader scope of concern. In a related incident, another presiding officer in Chikwawa allegedly attempted suicide after failing to reconcile manual and electronic tallies. Observers warned that unresolved issues across these cases could undermine public trust in the electoral process.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mwale |first=Joseph |date=2025-09-21 |title=CSOs demand speedy probe into electoral issues |url=https://mwnation.com/csos-demand-speedy-probe-into-electoral-issues/ |access-date=2025-09-21 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref>

On 21 September, MCP presidential runningmate [[Vitumbiko Mumba]] claimed during a press briefing that three men at Jenda roadblock had been following him for several days from the [[Mzuzu]] area with the intention of attacking him. However, Inspector General of Police Merlyn Yolamu stated that the police were not aware of the alleged incident.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-09-21 |title=Police IG ignorant of arrest in connection to Mumba's attack claim |url=https://www.malawivoice.com/2025/09/21/police-ig-ignorant-of-arrest-in-connection-to-mumbas-attack-claim/ |access-date=2025-09-22 |website=Malawi Voice |language=en-GB}}</ref> In the same briefing, MCP spokesperson [[Jessie Kabwila]] also alleged that during vote counting in some polling centres, the party's monitors were told to leave, with some reportedly being chased away and others injured.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Meki |first=Ntchindi |date=2025-09-22 |title=MCP makes vote rigging claims |url=https://mwnation.com/mcp-makes-vote-rigging-claims/ |access-date=2025-09-27 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref>

On 24 September, before announcing the winner, the commission stated that it had addressed all complaints received from political parties and stakeholders during and after the voting process, including those related to vote counting, data entry, and monitor access at polling centres. MEC chairperson [[Annabel Mtalimanja]] confirmed that investigations and corrective actions were carried out where necessary, and the official presidential results were gazetted on the same day to formalize the outcome of the election.<ref>{{cite web |last=Mlanjira |first=Duncan |title=MEC gazettes results of the presidential election including irregularities that Chakwera demanded and went to court for |url=https://www.maraviexpress.com/mec-gazettes-results-of-the-presidential-election-including-irregularities-that-chakwera-demanded-and-went-to-court-for/ |website=Malavi Express |date=25 September 2025 |access-date=26 September 2025}}</ref>

==Results==
===President===
Initial results suggested that former president [[Peter Mutharika]] of the [[Democratic Progressive Party (Malawi)|Democratic Progressive Party]] was leading in several areas, including the lakeshore districts of [[Nkhata Bay District|Nkhata Bay]] and [[Mangochi District|Mangochi]], in the presidential election.<ref>{{Cite web |work=Nyasa Times |date=17 September 2025 |title=Unofficial Results Show Considerable Lead for APM |url=https://www.nyasatimes.com/unofficial-results-show-considerable-lead-for-apm/ |access-date=17 September 2025 |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=17 September 2025 |title=Mutharika takes early lead as Malawi vote tallies trickle in |url=https://apanews.net/mutharika-takes-early-lead-as-malawi-vote-tallies-trickle-in/ |access-date=18 September 2025 |website=APAnews |language=en-US}}</ref> Official tallies were still awaited from the [[Malawi Electoral Commission]]. Despite this, both the MCP and the DPP claimed victory on 17 September.<ref>{{Cite web |author=Gregory Gondwe |date=19 September 2025 |title=Parties of both main candidates claim Malawi election victory but results not yet announced |url=https://apnews.com/article/malawi-elections-vote-africa-3bd61943304bc743db3adf4951e497f3 |access-date=19 September 2025 |website=AP News |language=en-US}}</ref> From 19 September, the commission began releasing official results, starting with four councils, among them [[Luchenza|Luchenza municipality]] and [[Likoma District|Likoma]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Owolabi |first=Adekunle |date=2025-09-20 |title=Malawi Electoral Commission releases partial presidential results |url=https://independentnews.co.sz/26320/politics/malawi-electoral-commission-releases-partial-presidential-results/ |access-date=2025-09-20 |website=Independent News Eswatini |language=en-ZA}}</ref>

On 18 September 2025 independent candidate Phunziro Mvula conceded defeat, stating that he accepted the outcome and pledged to run again in 2030.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Presidential Candidate Phunziro Mvula Concedes Defeat |url=https://zodiakmalawi.com/politics/presidential-candidate-phunziro-mvula-concedes-defeat |access-date=2025-09-20 |website=Zodiak Malawi}}</ref> The following day, Smart Swira also conceded defeat and urged Malawians to accept the results.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Naitha |first=Edgar |date=2025-09-19 |title=Smart Swira Becomes Second Presidential Candidate to Concede Defeat |url=https://www.faceofmalawi.com/2025/09/19/smart-swira-becomes-second-presidential-candidate-to-concede-defeat/ |work=Face of Malawi|access-date=2025-09-20 |language=en-US}}</ref> On 20 September, [[Kondwani Nankhumwa]] of the People's Development Party (PDP) and independent candidate Adil James Chilungo likewise conceded defeat, with Nankhumwa acknowledging that the preliminary results did not favour his party.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Richard |first=Mphatso Khutcha |date=2025-09-20 |title=Nankhumwa concedes defeat in 2025 presidential race |url=https://malawi24.com/2025/09/20/nankhumwa-concedes-defeat-in-2025-presidential-race/ |access-date=2025-09-20 |website=Malawi 24 |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-09-20 |title=Adil Chilungo accept defeat in 2025 presidential race |url=https://www.malawivoice.com/2025/09/20/adil-chilungo-accept-defeat-in-2025-presidential-race/ |access-date=2025-09-20 |website=Malawi Voice |language=en-GB}}</ref> On 21 September, [[United Transformation Movement|UTM]] presidential candidate [[Dalitso Kabambe]] conceded defeat and congratulated Peter Mutharika and the DPP, saying the unofficial results reflected the will of Malawians and urged a peaceful transfer of power.<ref>{{Cite web |last=PASUNGWI |first=JONATHAN |date=2025-09-22 |title=Kabambe congratulates APM, concedes defeat |url=https://mwnation.com/kabambe-congratulates-apm-concedes-defeat/ |access-date=2025-09-22 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref> These concessions were made before the commission had officially announced the winner of the presidential election.

On 23 September, partial results from 24 of Malawi's 36 electoral councils showed Mutharika leading the presidential vote with about 66% of valid ballots counted, with Chakwera trailing significantly.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-09-22 |title=Ex-President Mutharika leads Malawi presidential race with 66% of votes |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/ex-president-mutharika-leads-malawi-presidential-race-with-66-votes-2025-09-22/ |access-date=2025-09-23 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Mtemang’ombe |first=Eric |date=2025-09-23 |title=Mutharika builds commanding lead in presidential race |url=https://mwnation.com/mutharika-builds-commanding-lead-in-presidential-race/ |access-date=2025-09-23 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref> Former president [[Bakili Muluzi]] publicly asked Chakwera to accept the likely outcome.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-09-23 |title=Bakili tells Chakwera to pack his bags: "There is Life After State House" |url=https://www.malawivoice.com/2025/09/23/bakili-tells-chakwera-to-pack-his-bags-there-is-life-after-state-house/ |access-date=2025-09-23 |website=Malawi Voice |language=en-US}}</ref> The Malawi Council of Churches also appealed to Chakwera to concede defeat gracefully, noting that the electoral "table cannot change" in his favour.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-09-23 |title=Church urges Chakwera to concede defeat gracefully |url=https://malawi24.com/2025/09/23/church-urges-chakwera-to-concede-defeat-gracefully/ |access-date=2025-09-27 |website=Malawi 24 |language=en-GB}}</ref>

President Chakwera conceded defeat in a televised address on 24 September 2025, several hours before the commission had announced final results. At that point, only 24 of the 36 electoral councils were declared, yet Chakwera acknowledged that his opponent had achieved an insurmountable lead and urged Malawians to support a peaceful transition.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Munthali |first=Burnett |date=2025-09-24 |title=President Chakwera concedes defeat Ahead of official results in historic 2025 Malawi Election |url=https://malawi24.com/2025/09/24/president-chakwera-concedes-defeat-ahead-of-official-results-in-historic-2025-malawi-election/ |access-date=2025-09-24 |website=Malawi 24 |language=en-GB}}</ref>

Some analysts interpreted Mutharika's strong showing in the presidential contest as partly the product of a [[protest vote]] against the incumbent, citing public dissatisfaction over economic mismanagement, scarcity of goods, and weak decision-making by Chakwera's government. Experts also noted that votes that might otherwise have gone to UTM were diverted to Mutharika, reflecting strategic voting dynamics.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mvona |first=Ralph |date=2025-09-24 |title=APM benefited from protest vote—experts |url=https://mwnation.com/apm-benefited-from-protest-vote-experts/ |access-date=2025-09-24 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref>

