===National Assembly===
The commission began releasing official parliamentary results on 29 September, starting with 13 of the 36 electoral councils. Three constituencies, [[Mzimba South East Constituency|Mzimba South East]] and Nkhata Bay Central {{snd}} were withheld due to the need to examine [[Spoilt vote|voided]] votes, while in Nkhotakota Liwaladzi the parliamentary election was postponed following a [[court order]]. On the same day, the commission also announced that it had nullified the parliamentary results for the Lilongwe Mtandire{{snd}}Mtsiliza constituency over irregularities.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mpinganjira |first=Sosten |date=2025-09-29 |title=MEC nullifies Mtandire–Mtsiliza results over irregularities |work=Nthanda Times |url=https://nthandatimes.com/mec-nullifies-mtandire-mtsiliza-results-over-irregularities/ |access-date=2025-09-30 |language=en-US}}</ref> 

On 30 September, the commission announced results for 224 constituencies, with the DPP winning the largest share of seats.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Njiragoma |first=Wycliffe |date=2025-10-01 |title=DPP dominates parliamentary race |url=https://mwnation.com/dpp-dominates-parliamentary-race/ |access-date=2025-10-01 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref> It also pledged to conduct parliamentary [[by-election]]s in four constituencies where voting had been nullified or not completed.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kantayeni |first=Pempho |date=2025-09-30 |title=MEC to conduct parliamentary re-elections in four constituencies |url=https://www.maraviexpress.com/mec-to-conduct-parliamentary-re-elections-in-four-constituencies/ |access-date=2025-10-01 |website=Maravi Express |language=en-US}}</ref> In Lilongwe Chilobwe constituency, the commission had earlier declared MCP's Lawrence Chakakala Chaziya elected unopposed after no other candidate was nominated,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chavula |first=James |date=2025-08-20 |title=MP who won unopposed |url=https://mwnation.com/mp-who-won-unopposed/ |access-date=2025-10-02 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref> bringing the total constituencies to 229 as required.

The number of women in parliament increased from 40 to 48.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-10-16 |title=Malawi: Women in Parliament - Rising From 40 to 48 - Progress or Political Plateau? |url=https://allafrica.com/stories/202510160043.html |access-date=2025-11-16 |work=Nyasa Times |language=en}}</ref>

