== Aftermath ==
=== Voter turnout and electoral integrity ===
The 2025 general election saw a notable increase in voter turnout, with 5,502,982 individuals casting their ballots, representing 76.4% of the 7.2 million registered voters. This marks a significant rise from the 64.8% turnout in the [[2020 Malawian presidential election|2020 election]]. Analysts attribute this improvement to widespread public dissatisfaction with the prevailing economic conditions and renewed confidence in the electoral process, bolstered by reforms and increased transparency by MEC.<ref name="auto20">{{Cite web |last=Mhone |first=Macmillan |date=2025-09-26 |title=Turnout improves, but null-void votes worsen |url=https://mwnation.com/turnout-improves-but-null-void-votes-worsen/ |access-date=2025-09-26 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref> 

However, the election also experienced a rise in [[Spoilt vote|null and void]] votes. Approximately 155,225 votes were invalidated, accounting for 2.8% of the total votes cast. This figure more than doubles the 1.28% recorded in the 2020 election. Experts suggest that this increase may be due to insufficient voter education and the complexity introduced by having 17 presidential candidates on the ballot, which reduced the size of the voting boxes and potentially led to voter errors.<ref name="auto20" />
=== Inauguration and transition of power ===
[[Peter Mutharika]] was sworn in as [[president of Malawi]] on 4 October 2025 at [[Kamuzu Stadium]] in [[Blantyre]], in a ceremony attended by government officials, supporters, and several regional leaders, including Zimbabwean president [[Emmerson Mnangagwa]], Mozambican president [[Daniel Chapo]], and Botswanan president [[Duma Boko]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Malawi's Mutharika sworn in for second term as president |url=https://clubofmozambique.com/news/malawis-mutharika-sworn-in-for-second-term-as-president-293051/ |access-date=2025-10-09 |website=Mozambique |date=4 October 2025 |language=en-US}}</ref> Outgoing President [[Lazarus Chakwera]] did not attend the ceremony; however, the [[Malawi Congress Party|MCP]] issued a goodwill message wishing Mutharika success.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dzida |first=Rick |date=2025-10-04 |title=Absence of Chakwera at APM's inauguration ceremony is a missed opportunity |url=https://malawi24.com/2025/10/04/absence-of-chakwera-at-apms-inauguration-ceremony-is-a-missed-opportunity/ |access-date=2025-10-09 |website=Malawi 24 |language=en-GB}}</ref> 

In his inaugural address, Mutharika acknowledged that he was inheriting a country facing economic challenges such as high inflation, shortages of fuel and food, and debt constraints, and pledged a “tough and painful” return to work rather than promises of “milk and honey".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-10-05 |title=Malawi's President Mutharika brings hope as he vows to rebuild ailing economy |url=https://www.africanews.com/2025/10/05/malawis-president-mutharika-brings-hope-as-he-vows-to-rebuild-ailing-economy/ |access-date=2025-10-09 |website=Africanews |language=en}}</ref>

=== Government formation and appointments ===
Within days of taking office, President Mutharika named [[Joseph Mwanamveka]] as his [[Minister of Finance and Economic Affairs (Malawi)|finance minister]] - a role Mwanamveka had previously held between 2019 and 2020.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-10-06 |title=Malawi's president appoints economist Mwanamvekha as finance minister |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/malawis-president-appoints-economist-mwanamvekha-finance-minister-2025-10-06/ |access-date=2025-10-09 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref> In addition, he appointed [[Enoch Chihana]] as [[Second Vice-President of Malawi|second vice president]], [[George Chaponda]] as [[Minister of Foreign Affairs (Malawi)|foreign affairs minister]] and [[Alfred Gangata]] as minister of state.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-10-05 |title=Malawi Government Shake-Up: Mutharika Unveils New Cabinet |url=https://malawi24.com/2025/10/05/malawi-government-shake-up-mutharika-unveils-new-cabinet/ |access-date=2025-10-09 |website=Malawi 24 |language=en-GB}}</ref>

=== Debate over leader of the Opposition ===
With independents having emerged as the single largest bloc in the early unofficial results, some observers and commentators argued that the [[leader of the Opposition]] in the National Assembly should have come from among the independent MPs rather than from MCP. This was seen as a possible departure from precedent, given MCP’s traditional role as opposition leader, and was noted as a factor that could influence negotiations over parliamentary leadership roles.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mtenje |first=Dorica |date=2025-09-27 |title=Independent legislators dominate new Malawi Parliament: Where will leader of opposition, Speaker come from? |url=https://www.maravipost.com/independent-legislators-dominate-new-malawi-parliament-where-will-leader-of-opposition-speaker-come-from/ |access-date=2025-09-28 |website=The [[Maravi Post]] |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Reversal of headquarters relocations ===
On 10 October, President Mutharika issued an [[executive order]] reversing the earlier government directive that had moved several parastatal head offices from [[Blantyre]] and [[Zomba, Malawi|Zomba]] to [[Lilongwe]]. Under the new order, agencies including the [[Malawi Communications Regulatory Authority]], [[Malawi Electoral Commission]], and [[Malawi Housing Corporation]] were instructed to return to their original locations in Blantyre within three months, while the [[Malawi Prison Service]] was directed to relocate back to Zomba.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kasanda |first=Mathews |date=2025-10-14 |title=Executive order reverts Macra, others to Blantyre |url=https://mwnation.com/executive-order-reverts-macra-others-to-blantyre/ |access-date=2025-10-17 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Domestic reaction, political significance, and commentary ===
The transition of power in Malawi has been widely noted as a positive example of democratic turnover in a region often dominated by long-standing incumbents.<ref name="chatham">{{Cite web |date=2025-01-24 |title=Malawi's election result provides lessons for Africa |url=https://www.chathamhouse.org/2025/10/malawis-election-result-provides-lessons-africa |access-date=2025-10-09 |website=Chatham House |language=en}}</ref> Analysts have drawn attention to how the 2025 election was influenced heavily by economic discontent: with inflation persistently high, shortages of essential goods, and donor aid cuts, many voters appeared to use the election as a means to express dissatisfaction with the Chakwera government.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Savage |first=Rachel |date=2025-09-24 |title=Malawi's 85-year-old ex-leader returns to power in presidential election |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/sep/24/former-president-peter-mutharika-defeats-incumbent-in-malawi-presidential-election |access-date=2025-10-09 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Some commentators observed that the result suggests that party support in Malawi is not immutable, and that political comebacks are possible when incumbents mismanage economic or governance challenges.<ref name="chatham" />

==Court rulings==
On 11 September, five days before the polls, the High Court in Blantyre, presided over by Justice Chimbizgani Kacheche (Judicial Review Cause No. 19 of 2025), dismissed an opposition request for an independent audit of MEC’s election management system. The court upheld MEC’s decision to deny the audit but ruled that results could not be announced solely on the basis of electronic transmission and should await the arrival of manual tallies as well.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kasalika |first=Johnny |date=2025-09-12 |title=Court backs Mec on audit |url=https://mwnation.com/court-backs-mec-on-audit/ |access-date=2025-10-02 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Case Summary - The Democratic Progressive Party & 6 Others v Malawi Electoral Commission |url=https://judiciary.mw/node/790 |access-date=2025-10-02 |website=judiciary.mw}}</ref>

On 22 September, the High Court in Lilongwe dismissed an application by [[Madalitso Kazombo]] (former MCP MP and former Deputy Speaker in the last parliament) seeking permission for a judicial review of the [[Malawi Electoral Commission]]'s conduct in relation to the elections. The case (Judicial Review Cause No. 61 of 2025), heard by Justice Violet Palikena-Chipao, was dismissed on the grounds of procedural irregularities in the documents submitted, such as typographical errors, incorrect legal citations, unclear arguments and inconsistent facts. The court granted Kazombo liberty to refile with properly prepared documents.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chiputula |first=Eric |date=2025-09-23 |title=Court dismisses Kazombo's judicial review application against MEC |url=https://malawi24.com/2025/09/23/court-dismisses-kazombos-judicial-review-application-against-mec/ |access-date=2025-09-23 |website=Malawi 24 |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Madalitso Kazombo v MEC |url=https://www.judiciary.mw/sites/default/files/2025-09/Madalitso%20Kazombo%20v%20MEC.pdf |website=Judiciary Malawi |date=22 September 2025 |access-date= 23 September 2025}}</ref> 

On 23 September, President Chakwera and the governing MCP sought an [[injunction]] to restrain MEC from announcing the winner, but the High Court, presided over by Judge Howard Pemba, dismissed the application. However, the court allowed the claimants to pursue a judicial review of the matter,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mwale |first=Joseph |date=2025-09-24 |title=Court rebuffs Chakwera, MCP bid to block results |url=https://mwnation.com/court-rebuffs-chakwera-mcp-bid-to-block-results/ |access-date=2025-09-24 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Dr Chakwera & MCP v MEC case summary |url=https://www.judiciary.mw/sites/default/files/2025-09/Dr%20Chakwera%20%26%20MCP%20v%20MEC%20case%20summary.pdf |website=Judiciary Malawi |date=23 September 2025 |access-date= 24 September 2025}}</ref> although Chakwera later withdrew the case after conceding defeat.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-09-25 |title=Chakwera's Final Act of Statesmanship: Drops Court Battle After Conceding Defeat |url=https://www.nyasatimes.com/chakweras-final-act-of-statesmanship-drops-court-battle-after-conceding-defeat/ |access-date=2025-09-25 |work=Nyasa Times |language=en-GB}}</ref>

In the days following the announcement of parliamentary results, at least 83 election petitions were filed by losing candidates in constituencies across the country. These challenges spanned multiple districts, including [[Machinga District|Machinga]], [[Chikwawa District|Chikwawa]], [[Dowa District|Dowa]], and [[Kasungu District|Kasungu]], and contested outcomes in several parliamentary seats. The High Court heard the cases, marking one of the most extensive waves of post-election litigation in Malawi’s recent history.<ref>{{Cite web |last=PASUNGWI |first=JONATHAN |date=2025-10-15 |title=Mec faces 83 court petitions |url=https://mwnation.com/mec-faces-83-court-petitions/ |access-date=2025-10-15 |website=Nation Online |language=en-US}}</ref>

